1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,956
One planet on this list runs so hot, atoms cannot bond into molecules.

2
00:00:04,956 --> 00:00:09,580
On another, radiation would kill you before your boots ever touched the ground.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:14,841
Astronomers have confirmed more than five thousand planets beyond our solar system.

4
00:00:14,841 --> 00:00:17,602
Not one has ever been photographed up close.

5
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:22,088
We know they are real from something more reliable than a picture: physics.

6
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:29,330
These are eight of the most hostile planets ever confirmed, ranked by how fast each one would kill you.

7
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:32,219
Every discovery is real, and every number is sourced.

8
00:00:33,366 --> 00:00:37,247
And the planet at number one was not just found to be lethal.

9
00:00:37,247 --> 00:00:41,427
It was one of the very first planets ever discovered outside our solar system.

10
00:00:41,427 --> 00:00:43,815
Before it kills you, it already made history.

11
00:00:44,933 --> 00:00:46,940
One honest rule before we start.

12
00:00:46,940 --> 00:00:51,288
Nobody has ever photographed the surface of a planet outside our solar system.

13
00:00:51,288 --> 00:00:56,974
Every alien surface you see in this video is a labeled scientific illustration, built from real data.

14
00:00:56,974 --> 00:01:00,319
The telescopes, the discoveries, and the numbers are all real.

15
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:02,223
Number eight.

16
00:01:03,366 --> 00:01:14,520
Thirty one light years away is a world called Gliese four three six b — a planet about the size of Neptune, orbiting so close to its star that one full year there lasts just two and a half days.

17
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:20,032
Its surface runs near eight hundred degrees Fahrenheit, hot enough to soften lead.

18
00:01:20,032 --> 00:01:29,772
And yet astronomers say much of this planet may still be locked as solid water ice, crushed into that shape not by cold, but by its own bone-crushing gravity.

19
00:01:30,899 --> 00:01:40,303
It was confirmed using the MEarth Project — a real network of small ground telescopes built specifically to watch dim red dwarf stars for the flicker of a passing planet.

20
00:01:41,433 --> 00:01:43,098
Nobody has photographed its surface.

21
00:01:43,098 --> 00:01:50,759
Everything you saw of the ice itself was a scientific illustration — the temperature, the mass, and the telescope are the real part.

22
00:01:51,899 --> 00:01:52,813
Number seven.

23
00:01:53,933 --> 00:01:58,265
TrES two b reflects less than one percent of the starlight that hits it.

24
00:01:58,265 --> 00:02:01,979
That makes it darker than coal, and darker than black acrylic paint.

25
00:02:03,099 --> 00:02:13,783
It was caught by NASA's Kepler space telescope, measuring a brightness dip of just six parts per million — at the time, the smallest signal ever detected from a planet outside our solar system.

26
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:23,606
Astronomers think its dayside glows a dull ember red from its own heat, absorbing almost all incoming light with no reflective clouds to lighten it at all.

27
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:25,882
Number six.

28
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,158
Fifty five Cancri e circles its star once every seventeen point seven hours — closer than Mercury circles our own Sun.

29
00:02:34,158 --> 00:02:40,636
Its permanent dayside runs near twenty seven hundred kelvin, hot enough to keep an entire hemisphere as standing lava.

30
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:44,594
Astronomers once proposed its interior was one third diamond.

31
00:02:44,594 --> 00:02:52,764
A closer look at its host star's own chemistry made that claim far less certain — it is a genuinely contested idea, not a settled fact.

32
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:02,494
It was studied using NASA's Spitzer Space Telescope, watching its temperature swing by hundreds of degrees between its permanent day side and its permanent night side.

33
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:04,547
Number five.

34
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:15,070
HD one eight nine seven three three b is a cobalt blue gas giant, sixty four light years away — and Hubble measured that blue directly, from the planet's own light.

35
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,096
That blue is not an ocean.

36
00:03:18,096 --> 00:03:25,996
It is a sky full of silicate cloud, the same mineral family as glass, torn sideways by winds near fifty four hundred miles an hour.

37
00:03:27,133 --> 00:03:37,086
Its own star is a flare prone dwarf, caught by astronomers mid blast — a real stellar flare, actively stripping the planet's upper atmosphere away into space.

38
00:03:38,233 --> 00:03:41,125
The color is real, measured by a real telescope.

39
00:03:41,125 --> 00:03:47,872
What is illustrated is only the close up view of the clouds themselves — no camera has ever gotten that close.

40
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:49,993
Number four.

41
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:58,369
WASP seventy six b runs above twenty four hundred degrees Celsius on its dayside, hot enough to vaporize iron into gas.

42
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:13,293
Supersonic winds drag that iron vapor toward the night side, where astronomers using the Very Large Telescope in Chile watched it vanish from the gas phase, in twenty twenty — exactly what you would expect if it were condensing back into solid metal.

43
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:19,840
Their own conclusion, published in the journal Nature: on this planet, it rains iron.

44
00:04:20,966 --> 00:04:24,336
The detection is real data, from a real telescope.

45
00:04:24,336 --> 00:04:32,199
The image of iron actually falling as rain is, again, an illustration — nobody has flown a probe through those clouds.

46
00:04:33,333 --> 00:04:34,247
Number three.

47
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:46,520
WASP twelve b is a gas giant so close to its star that gravity has stretched it into an egg shape — and the star is stripping its atmosphere away, atom by atom, into a glowing stream of stolen gas.

48
00:04:47,666 --> 00:04:49,525
Its orbit is measurably shrinking.

49
00:04:49,525 --> 00:04:58,820
Current estimates give it roughly three million years before it spirals into its star completely — an unusually short amount of time, by astronomical standards.

50
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:10,493
It was found using SuperWASP, a real network of robotic telescopes in the Canary Islands and South Africa, built to scan the whole sky for the tiny dimming a transiting planet causes.

51
00:05:11,633 --> 00:05:18,441
The egg shape, the gas stream, and the slow death spiral are all measured from real light, not photographed directly.

52
00:05:18,441 --> 00:05:22,866
Everything you see of the planet itself is an illustration of that data.

53
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:24,836
Number two.

54
00:05:25,966 --> 00:05:31,608
KELT nine b's dayside runs about forty six hundred kelvin, hotter than most stars in the galaxy.

55
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:35,228
At that heat, molecules cannot even survive.

56
00:05:35,228 --> 00:05:44,854
Hydrogen gas rips apart into loose atoms, and metals like iron and titanium float free as bare vapor, instead of ever bonding into a molecule at all.

57
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,967
It completes one orbit of its star in under thirty six hours, close enough that its swollen atmosphere is slowly being stripped away into space.

58
00:05:55,100 --> 00:06:04,817
It was found by KELT, a real pair of small automated telescopes in Arizona and South Africa, cheap enough that a university team could scan the whole sky with them.

59
00:06:05,966 --> 00:06:11,003
Before number one — one planet you might expect on this list isn't here.

60
00:06:11,003 --> 00:06:16,400
Tabby's Star, famous for its mysterious dimming, was once linked to speculation about alien megastructures.

61
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:22,876
Real follow up data points to orbiting dust and comet fragments instead, and NASA has never suggested otherwise.

62
00:06:22,876 --> 00:06:25,035
This list only counts what's confirmed.

63
00:06:26,166 --> 00:06:27,080
Number one.

64
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:33,549
In nineteen ninety two, astronomers confirmed the very first planets ever found outside our solar system.

65
00:06:33,549 --> 00:06:36,559
They do not orbit a star like our sun.

66
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:44,027
They orbit a pulsar, the crushed, spinning corpse of a star that already exploded once.

67
00:06:44,027 --> 00:06:49,089
Astronomers nicknamed the pulsar Lich, and its planets Draugr, Poltergeist, and Phobetor.

68
00:06:50,233 --> 00:06:54,946
The pulsar fires X-rays and a wind of high speed particles across all three worlds.

69
00:06:54,946 --> 00:07:01,231
Land on one, and the radiation would very likely kill a human being before their boots ever touched the ground.

70
00:07:02,366 --> 00:07:13,285
The discovery came from the real, three hundred and five meter Arecibo radio telescope in Puerto Rico, measuring anomalies in pulsar timing just milliseconds wide, over three years of observation.

71
00:07:14,433 --> 00:07:17,341
Astronomers still don't fully know how these planets formed.

72
00:07:17,341 --> 00:07:24,128
The leading idea is that they are second generation worlds, built from wreckage after the star that made this pulsar died.

73
00:07:24,128 --> 00:07:25,744
It remains an open question.

74
00:07:26,866 --> 00:07:35,295
Every planet on this list is real, confirmed, and published — the telescopes, the scientists, and the numbers are all sourced in the description.

75
00:07:35,295 --> 00:07:42,670
Where you saw a planet's surface up close, that was always a labeled illustration, because that photograph does not exist yet.

76
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:53,098
And if these planets convinced you that space itself is hostile enough, wait until you see the place on Earth that's scarier than space — that story is on your screen right now.

77
00:07:53,098 --> 00:07:57,044
Subscribe to mindwired, so you don't miss what's hiding in the next system over.

